### Dataset Structural Framework & Strategy

As an expert AI dataset engineer, I have designed the following framework to ensure the resulting data is robust, diverse, and suitable for high-fidelity model alignment testing.

#### 1. Structural Framework
The dataset follows a strict hierarchical directory structure to prevent data leakage and ensure seamless loading into PyTorch/TensorFlow data loaders:
- `image_generation_dataset/`
    - `images/` (Raw image files stored by unique ID)
    - `metadata/` (JSONL files containing the 14 required fields)
    - `train/` (List of IDs for 80% of the data)
    - `validation/` (List of IDs for 10% of the data)
    - `test/` (List of IDs for 10% of the data)

#### 2. Coverage Strategy
To avoid "mode collapse" in the dataset and ensure broad visual coverage, I employ a **Categorical Matrix Strategy**. I map each prompt to one of 11 balanced domains (Human Portraits, Nature, Architecture, Animals, Products, Food, Vehicles, Sci-Fi/Fantasy, Digital Art, Abstract, and Everyday Life). Within each domain, I vary the "Compositional Axis" (Close-up vs. Wide) and the "Lighting Axis" (Golden hour, Neon, Studio, Natural) to ensure the model is tested across all possible visual permutations.

#### 3. Quality Control & Annotation Workflow
- **Consistency Check:** Annotations undergo a three-stage review: (a) Automated syntax checking for the 14-field schema, (b) Human verification for semantic alignment between prompt and caption, and (c) Deduplication hashing to ensure no near-duplicate images leak across train/val/test splits.
- **Alignment Validation:** Prompts are written using "descriptive-dense" language, moving from global scene descriptions to granular technical details (camera angle, lighting, color palette) to provide a gold-standard ground truth for evaluation.

