### Strategic Overview for High-Quality Text-to-Image Dataset Construction

As a senior machine learning data engineer, I propose the following framework to ensure robustness, diversity, and reproducibility for your generative model training.

#### 1. Proposed Dataset Structure
To facilitate efficient loading and version control, the dataset will follow a strict hierarchical directory structure:
```
image_generation_dataset/
├── images/
│   ├── train/
│   ├── validation/
│   └── test/
├── metadata/
│   ├── train.jsonl
│   ├── validation.jsonl
│   └── test.jsonl
└── README.md (containing license info and schema definitions)
```
This separation ensures that preprocessing pipelines can easily map image files to their corresponding metadata entries without complex parsing logic.

#### 2. Generation Strategy
Our generation strategy focuses on **balanced category coverage** and **prompt diversity**. We will target 11 distinct visual domains: human subjects/portraits, nature/landscapes, architecture/interiors, animals/wildlife, objects/product photography, food/beverages, vehicles/transportation, fantasy/sci-fi, digital art/illustrations, abstract compositions, and everyday life scenarios. 

To prevent mode collapse during training, we will enforce a distribution where no single category dominates. Prompts will be engineered to vary in complexity, from simple subject descriptions to intricate scenes involving specific lighting, camera angles, and artistic styles.

#### 3. Quality-Control Process
- **Duplicate Detection**: We will use perceptual hashing (pHash) and semantic embedding similarity (CLIP-based) to identify and remove near-duplicate images across all splits, ensuring no data leakage between training, validation, and test sets.
- **Split Integrity**: The dataset will be partitioned into **80% Training**, **10% Validation**, and **10% Testing**. This split will be performed *after* deduplication to guarantee independence.
- **Metadata Validation**: Every entry will be validated against a strict schema requiring 14 specific fields. Entries missing fields or containing contradictory tags (e.g., "night" lighting with "bright sunlight") will be flagged for review.
- **Bias Mitigation**: We will audit the "human subjects" category for demographic balance and the "art style" field for overrepresentation of specific popular models (e.g., excessive "anime" vs. "photorealistic").

