### Dataset Engineering Framework: Text-to-Image Reproducibility

As an expert AI dataset engineer and computer vision specialist, my objective is to eliminate "semantic drift" and "mode collapse" by ensuring the dataset is balanced across diverse visual domains. 

#### 1. Proposed Dataset Structure
The dataset will be organized into a hierarchical folder structure to support standard PyTorch/TensorFlow data loaders and ensure reproducible training:
- `image_generation_dataset/`
    - `images/` (Raw high-resolution assets)
    - `metadata/` (JSONL files containing the 14 required fields)
    - `train/` (80% - High-density diversity)
    - `validation/` (10% - Distributional check)
    - `test/` (10% - Out-of-distribution/Generalization check)

#### 2. Generation Strategy for Model Performance
To optimize the latent space of the generative model, I will employ:
- **Combinatorial Sampling:** Ensuring every "Main Subject" is paired with multiple "Lighting Conditions" and "Art Styles" to decouple subject from style.
- **Compositional Variance:** Utilizing a mix of extreme close-ups, wide shots, and dynamic angles to prevent the model from defaulting to center-weighted compositions.
- **Semantic Density:** Prompts will follow a "Subject -> Action -> Environment -> Technical Specification" hierarchy to improve prompt adherence.

#### 3. Quality Control & Alignment Evaluation
- **Cross-Split Leakage Check:** I will use perceptual hashing (pHash) to ensure no near-duplicate images exist across the train, validation, and test splits.
- **Label Fidelity:** Metadata will be cross-referenced against the generated prompt to ensure the "Main Subject" and "Color Palette" fields are logically consistent.
- **Diversity Metric:** Calculation of the "Unique Token Ratio" across the dataset to ensure the model doesn't overfit to a small set of adjectives.

