### 1. Proposed Dataset Structure
The dataset will follow a strict hierarchical folder structure to ensure compatibility with standard training pipelines (e.g., Diffusers, DreamBooth):
```
image_generation_dataset/
├── images/
│   ├── train/
│   ├── validation/
│   └── test/
└── metadata/
    ├── train.jsonl
    ├── validation.jsonl
    └── test.jsonl
```
This separation ensures that no image data leaks between splits. The metadata files will contain one JSON object per line (JSONL), linking image filenames to their rich textual annotations.

### 2. Generation Strategy
To maximize model generalization, we will employ a stratified sampling approach across 11 balanced categories: Human Subjects, Nature, Architecture, Animals, Objects, Food, Vehicles, Fantasy, Digital Art, Abstract, and Everyday Life. 
- **Prompt Engineering**: Prompts will be constructed using a modular template: `[Subject] + [Action/Context] + [Environment] + [Style] + [Lighting] + [Camera]`. This ensures semantic richness.
- **Split Allocation**: We will enforce an 80/10/10 split (Training/Validation/Test). Crucially, we will use semantic hashing on prompts and perceptual hashing (pHash) on generated images to ensure near-duplicates do not appear in multiple splits.

### 3. Quality-Control Process
- **Duplicate Detection**: Any image with a pHash distance < 5 from an image in another split will be discarded or reassigned.
- **Annotation Verification**: All 14 required metadata fields will be validated via schema checks. Missing fields or generic tags (e.g., "image", "photo") will trigger a re-generation of the annotation.
- **Resolution Standardization**: All entries will target 1024x1024 or 1024x768 resolutions to align with modern SDXL architectures, though legacy 512x512 variants can be included for specific fine-tuning tasks.

