### Hyperspectral Imaging Dataset Strategy

#### 1. Dataset Structure
The dataset is organized to support deep learning workflows for spectral analysis and image-to-image translation. It follows a hierarchical folder structure rooted at `image_generation_dataset/`, containing subdirectories for `images/`, `metadata/`, `train/`, `validation/`, and `test/`. The dataset is split into 80% Training, 10% Validation, and 10% Testing to prevent near-duplicate leakage across splits.

#### 2. Prompting Strategy for Non-Visible Light Signatures
Since standard AI generators operate in RGB, we simulate hyperspectral data using False Color Composites (FCC). We explicitly define which non-visible bands map to RGB channels (e.g., NIR to Red, Red to Green, Green to Blue). We use spectral feature keywords like "chlorophyll absorption pit," "water-band absorption," and "spectral signature" to guide the model toward specific textures and contrast levels. Prompts also specify the sensor type to influence simulated noise and spatial resolution.

#### 3. Quality Control for Spectral Accuracy
To ensure generated images are scientifically plausible, we compare generated signatures against the USGS Spectral Library using the Spectral Angle Mapper (SAM) algorithm. We apply simulated atmospheric corrections to ensure consistent lighting across the dataset. Finally, we calculate the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) between the generated false-color intensity and the known reflectance curves of the target species.

