### Dataset Proposal: Temporal Street Photography Analysis (TSPA)

#### 1. Proposed Dataset Structure
The dataset is designed to facilitate a comparative learning objective, allowing the model to distinguish between the aesthetic markers of the 1950s (analog, film-based, specific social cues) and the 2020s (digital, high-dynamic range, modern technology).

**Folder Hierarchy:**
```text
image_generation_dataset/
├── images/                 # All raw generated assets
├── metadata/                # JSONL files containing the 14 required fields
├── train/                  # 80% of the dataset (split by category and era)
├── validation/             # 10% of the dataset (held-out for hyperparameter tuning)
└── test/                   # 10% of the dataset (final evaluation of era-detection)
```

#### 2. Generation Strategy
To prevent "mode collapse" where the model only learns stereotypes, I will employ a **Cross-Category Temporal Mapping** strategy. For every modern street scene, a historical equivalent is generated. 
- **1950s Logic:** Focus on "Silver Halide" characteristics, mid-century fashion, analog signage, and limited color palettes (Kodachrome/Tri-X).
- **2020s Logic:** Focus on "CMOS Sensor" characteristics, smartphones, LED lighting, contemporary urbanism, and high-frequency detail.

#### 3. Quality-Control (QC) Process for Period Accuracy
**A. Grain and Texture Control:**
- **1950s:** Implement a "Gaussian Noise + Perlin Noise" overlay to simulate organic film grain. Specify "heavy silver halide grain" for B&W and "fine-grain emulsion" for color.
- **2020s:** Implement "Digital Noise" (ISO noise) in low-light scenes and "Perfectly Clean" planes for daylight, simulating modern noise-reduction algorithms.

**B. Color Grading:**
- **1950s:** Apply a "Shifted Yellow/Cyan" cast for aged prints and "Saturated Reds/Teals" for Kodachrome emulation. Contrast is set to "Soft/Low" to mimic analog latitude.
- **2020s:** Apply "Rec. 709" or "P3" color spaces with high contrast, deep blacks, and "Neon/Cyan/Magenta" highlights to simulate modern LED and OLED environments.

