### Dataset Engineering Proposal: High-Fashion Editorial Photography (Avant-Garde Textiles)

#### 1. Dataset Structure
The dataset will be organized into a hierarchical structure to ensure seamless integration into PyTorch/TensorFlow data loaders. The root directory `image_generation_dataset/` will contain:
- `images/`: Raw high-resolution image files stored in split-specific subfolders (e.g., `images/train/`).
- `metadata/`: JSONL files containing the 14 required metadata fields.
- `train/`: 80% of the data, focusing on diversity and breadth of textile types.
- `validation/`: 10% of the data, focusing on edge cases of fabric physics.
- `test/`: 10% of the data, held-out set for final FID/CLIP score evaluation.

#### 2. Generation Strategy
To avoid "model collapse" and ensure high-fidelity textile rendering, the strategy employs:
- **Textile Specialization:** Prompts explicitly define weave, weight, and light-interaction (e.g., "iridescent organza," "sculptural neoprene").
- **Compositional Variety:** A mix of full-body editorial shots, extreme close-ups of fabric grains, and architectural silhouettes.
- **Lighting Rigor:** Use of professional studio terminology (e.g., "butterfly lighting," "clamshell lighting") to ensure the AI learns the relationship between light and texture.
- **Balanced Categories:** While focusing on high-fashion editorial aesthetics, prompts will span 11 visual categories (Human, Nature, Architecture, Animals, Objects, Food, Vehicles, Fantasy, Digital, Abstract, Everyday) to ensure model robustness.

#### 3. Quality Control Process
- **Deduplication:** Using perceptual hashing (pHash) to ensure no near-duplicates exist across splits.
- **Textile Validation:** Manual review to ensure "avant-garde" elements are structurally coherent and not mere glitches.
- **Metadata Alignment:** Cross-referencing prompts with generated labels to ensure 1:1 mapping of lighting and camera specs. Automated checks verify the presence of all 14 required fields and category balance.

